To solve the problem, I will perform an in-order traversal of the binary search tree. During the traversal, I will check if the current node's identifier lies within the specified range [k1, k2]. If it does, I will add it to the result list. The traversal will stop when the current node exceeds k2, ensuring that we only explore the relevant parts of the tree. This approach is efficient because we only visit nodes that are necessary to find the identifiers in the specified range, resulting in a time complexity of O(h + s), where h is the height of the tree and s is the number of identifiers found. 

Here is the Python code for the algorithm:

```python
def range_search_bst(root, k1, k2):
    result = []
    def inorder_traversal(node):
        if not node:
            return
        if k1 < node.identifier:
            inorder_traversal(node.left)
        if k1 <= node.identifier <= k2:
            result.append(node.identifier)
        if k2 > node.identifier:
            inorder_traversal(node.right)
    inorder_traversal(root)
    return result
